      "content": "thanks. looking at the preliminary regression, the ethnicity coefficients are coming back surprisingly high—way higher than our socioeconomic variables like median income. it almost looks like the sheer increase in the minority population percentage is a better predictor of larceny than the actual poverty levels. in your experience with actuarial datasets, is it common to see this kind of direct correlation where ethnic shifts act as a primary driver for crime risk, or is there something else we should be looking for to explain why the diversity metric is so dominant?",
